.TH OSFP\-COMPARE 1 2026-09-23 osfp "User Commands"
.SH NAME
osfp\-compare \- report how the filesystem drifted from a fingerprint
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B osfp compare
.B \-b
.I FILE
.RB [ \-\-show
.IR CODES ]
.RB [ \-\-ignore\-from
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-new\-dir\-mode
.IR MODE ]
.RB [ \-\-format
.IR FORMAT ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-exclude
.IR PATH ]...\&
.RB [ \-\-jobs
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-fail\-on\-diff ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-color ]
.RB [ \-\-allow\-os\-mismatch ]
.RB [ \-\-allow\-non\-root ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B osfp compare
verifies a fingerprint written by
.BR osfp\-baseline (1),
scans the filesystem again over exactly the perimeter the fingerprint
recorded \(em the same root, exclusions, mount policy and depth limit \(em and
reports every difference.
It must run as root, and refuses a fingerprint whose key is not the key of the
running system.
.PP
The scan and the fingerprint are merged in a single pass, in the same order,
so a comparison takes about as long as the baseline did, and keeps only the
fingerprint's list of directories in memory.
.PP
A directory the fingerprint does not know is reported once, with counters,
and its contents are neither listed nor hashed; see
.BR \-\-new\-dir\-mode .
.PP
The modification time is never compared: a
.BR touch ,
a restore from backup or an identical reinstall produces no line at all.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-b " FILE"
The fingerprint to compare against.
Required.
.TP
.BI \-\-show " CODES"
Report only these change codes, comma separated, for instance
.BR "\(tiF,!T" .
The others are still counted, and the summary says how many were hidden.
.TP
.BI \-\-ignore\-from " FILE"
Drop the changes matched by the rules in
.IR FILE ;
see
.BR "IGNORE RULES" .
.TP
.BI \-\-new\-dir\-mode " MODE"
What to report below a directory the fingerprint does not know:
.B summary
(the default) reports the directory with the number of files and
directories below it and their total size, without hashing anything;
.B deep
lists and hashes everything below it;
.B skip
reports the directory alone.
.TP
.BI \-\-format " FORMAT"
.B text
(the default) or
.BR json ;
see
.BR "REPORT FORMATS" .
.TP
.BI \-o " FILE"
Write the report to
.I FILE
instead of standard output, atomically; the file is left out of the scan.
Like a fingerprint, it is created with mode 0600: it lists paths and hashes
of the system.
.TP
.BI \-\-exclude " PATH"
Leave
.I PATH
out of this scan, on top of the fingerprint's own exclusions.
Repeatable.
Whatever the fingerprint holds under
.I PATH
is then reported as deleted, and the report header warns about it.
.TP
.BI \-\-jobs " N"
Hash with
.I N
workers.
The default is the number of CPUs, at most 8.
.TP
.B \-\-fail\-on\-diff
Exit with status 1 when differences are found outside the volatile
directories; see
.BR "EXPECTED NOISE" .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Never colour the text report.
It is coloured only when standard output is a terminal, and never when
.B NO_COLOR
is set.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-os\-mismatch
Compare even though the key of the running system differs from the
fingerprint's.
Most of the filesystem will then be reported as changed.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-non\-root
Scan without root privileges.
Only a fingerprint that was itself taken unprivileged can be compared this
way: the other combination would report every unreadable path as deleted.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Report progress on standard error while scanning.
.SH CHANGE CODES
.TP
.B +D
directory added
.TP
.B \-D
directory deleted
.TP
.B \(tiD
directory whose mode, owner or group changed
.TP
.B +F
path added
.TP
.B \-F
path deleted
.TP
.B \(tiF
file whose content changed, by SHA\-256
.TP
.B %F
file with the same content but a different mode, owner or group
.TP
.B !T
type changed, for instance a file that became a symbolic link
.TP
.B \(tiL
symbolic link with a different target
.TP
.B ?E
path that could not be read
.SH IGNORE RULES
An ignore file holds one rule per line; blank lines and lines starting with
.B #
are skipped.
A rule is a pattern, with the syntax of
.B \-\-exclude
in
.BR osfp\-baseline (1),
optionally preceded by change codes and a colon:
.PP
.RS
.nf
/var/lib/myapp          every change under this path
\(tiF,%F:/etc/adjtime       only these codes, for this path
*.pyc                   every change to a file with this name
.fi
.RE
.PP
Rules apply after classification: an ignored change is still counted, and the
summary says how many were hidden.
Unlike an exclusion, a rule never narrows the scan.
.SH EXPECTED NOISE
Changes under the volatile directories are reported under a heading of their
own, at the end of the text report, rather than among the findings:
.BR /var/log ,
.B /var/cache
and
.BR /var/spool ;
on Solaris and illumos also
.BR /var/adm ,
.BR /var/svc/log ,
.B /var/audit
and, on Solaris 11,
.BR /var/share/adm ,
.B /var/share/audit
and
.BR /var/share/sstore ;
on macOS
.B /private/var/log
and
.B /private/var/folders
instead of the common list.
.PP
They are scanned and counted like everything else, and the summary says how
many of the changes they account for, but they never make
.B \-\-fail\-on\-diff
exit with status 1: on a running system, a log grows between any two scans.
.PP
The audit trail is among them.
A deleted audit file is therefore reported, but does not by itself fail
.BR \-\-fail\-on\-diff .
.SH REPORT FORMATS
The text report groups the changes by code, one section per code with its
count, then the expected noise, then a summary: the count for every code, how
many changes were expected noise, hidden by
.B \-\-show
or by ignore rules, how many entries were scanned, and every mount point that
was not crossed, with its type.
It is held until the end, because each section heading carries a count.
.PP
With
.BR "\-\-format json" ,
the report is JSON Lines: a header object, one object per change, then a
summary object, each with a
.B record
field saying which.
A change carries its code, its path and, as
.B old
and
.BR new ,
everything the fingerprint and the scan know about the entry, modification
time included.
The summary carries the counts, a
.B total
and the part of it that was
.BR expected .
The stream is written as the scan goes: on standard output it can be piped to
.BR jq (1)
while the scan runs, whereas with
.B \-o
the file appears under its name only once complete.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B 0
when nothing but expected noise was found, or without
.BR \-\-fail\-on\-diff ;
.B 1
when
.B \-\-fail\-on\-diff
found other differences;
.B 2
on error, including a fingerprint that fails verification or whose key does
not match;
.B 3
on a privilege mismatch;
.B 130
when interrupted.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
# osfp compare \-b linux\-debian\-12\-amd64.osfp
# osfp compare \-b linux\-debian\-12\-amd64.osfp \-\-show \(aq\(tiF,!T\(aq \-\-ignore\-from ignore.txt
# osfp compare \-b linux\-debian\-12\-amd64.osfp \-\-format json \-o drift.jsonl
# jq \-r \(aqselect(.record == "change" and .op == "\(tiF") | .path\(aq drift.jsonl
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR osfp (1),
.BR osfp\-baseline (1),
.BR jq (1)
